messages:
olivia: Heads-up — multiple customers reporting duplicate POSTs after retries when using our TS SDK + undici. Repro attached.

kai: Can you drop the minimal repro + node/undici/sdk versions?

olivia: Node 18.17, undici 5.14.0, @redwood/sdk 1.9.2
olivia: Steps:
1) call sdk.client.post(...) which uses global fetch
2) network hiccup -> abort triggered
3) sdk retry kicks in, but server logs show two POSTs completed
olivia: Here's a trimmed repro (TS):
```ts
// simplified repro showing signal reuse between attempts
const controller = new AbortController();
await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload), signal: controller.signal });
// SDK aborts controller on timeout then retries using the same signal object
```

rachel: Hmm — looks like an AbortSignal reuse between attempts. undici's Request/Body cloning behavior needs a fresh signal per fetch. If the SDK reuses the same controller across retries, undici can have a timing window where a request is aborted but still completes at the backend.

kai: Can you paste the exact SDK retry config? e.g., maxRetries, backoff, idempotency behavior?

olivia: default retries: 3, exponential backoff starting 250ms, no Idempotency-Key set. We're seeing dupes only on POSTs with non-idempotent endpoints.

alex: Short-term workaround: set an Idempotency-Key header per request in the app. Also ensure the SDK creates a fresh AbortController for each attempt. Example fix: 
```ts
async function sendWithRetries(req) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < maxRetries; attempt++) {
    const c = new AbortController();
    try {
      return await fetch(req.url, { ...req, signal: c.signal });
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt === maxRetries - 1) throw err;
      await sleep(backoff(attempt));
    }
  }
}
```

rachel: We're tracking a bug where the SDK's retry wrapper reuses the original signal when it should create a new one. This can cause undici to reuse connection/body state and lead to "in-flight but aborted" requests that nonetheless reach the backend. Fix in PR #4123 — patch will be in the next patch release. ETA: EOD tomorrow.

olivia: Nice. Can we also detect non-idempotent methods and skip automatic retries unless Idempotency-Key present? Customers expect POST not retried blindly.

kai: Agreed. As mitigation we added a config flag `retryNonIdempotent: false` in the affected tenant override.

support-bot: Ticket RED-7891 created for customers A/B and C (escalation). Assigned to rachel. :spiral_notepad:

alex: Also include note for ops: if you see duplicated request IDs, check for missing `x-idempotency-key`. Recommend logging raw request headers + request body hash to correlate duplicates. Added to internal runbook.

olivia: Will inform customers and recommend sdk bump + idempotency-key. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:

rachel: Patch published: please test with @redwood/sdk 1.9.3 which creates a fresh AbortController per retry and disables retries for non-idempotent methods by default. Changelog: https://redwood-inference.internal/releases/1.9.3

kai: Closing loop — customers confirmed mitigation reduced duplicates to zero after adding Idempotency-Key and upgrading.
